However, relying solely on `mb_internal_encoding()` can sometimes be brittle if the source string is actually coming from a file or database in a different encoding. In those scenarios, you must use `mb_convert_encoding()` to normalize the string first. For instance, if `$var` was read as `ISO-8859-1` but contains Cyrillic, converting it to UTF-8 immediately prevents corruption downstream.

```php
// If the source might not be UTF-8, normalize it first
$normalized = mb_convert_encoding($var, 'UTF-8', 'Auto'); 
$foo = mb_substr($normalized, 0, 142);
```

There is also a trade-off to consider when dealing with very long strings or high-performance requirements. While `mb_substr()` is safer, it can be slightly slower than native `substr()` because of the internal encoding checks and byte manipulation logic. If performance profiling shows that string slicing is a bottleneck in a high-throughput API endpoint, you might consider using `substr()` but only after verifying that the entire input pipeline is strictly UTF-8 compliant. In such cases, adding error handling to catch invalid UTF-8 sequences becomes part of your data validation strategy rather than just a coding preference.

For developers working within the Laravel ecosystem, this issue often surfaces when parsing user-generated content or handling legacy database fields. If you are building an application where text integrity is criticalâsuch as processing legal documents or financial reportsâyou should treat string slicing as a potential source of data corruption. Ensuring that your application's default encoding matches your storage layer and that you explicitly set the internal encoding before calling multibyte functions is a fundamental practice. For more details on handling encoding complexities in modern PHP applications, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide further context on best practices for string manipulation within web frameworks.

The core takeaway remains simple: never assume `substr()` works on characters. Always verify the encoding state of your data and use `mb_substr()` with an explicit internal encoding setting to ensure you are slicing logical characters, not arbitrary bytes.
